# Project — Milestone 1 charter (shipped; preserved verbatim)

Moved out of the boot-inlined `project.prompt` on 2026-08-30 to keep the
stable boot prefix inside its 44000-char budget (BL-859/BL-1227). Nothing is
reworded or dropped: every sentence below is the human's original Milestone-1
charter, exactly as it stood. It is HISTORY — Milestone 1 shipped long ago and
several of its "do NOT build these yet" items (phone app, chat integration,
cost tracking, model/backend switching) have since been delivered as their own
milestones. Read it for provenance, never as current scope.

Two constraints it carried are still LIVE and are still stated in the
boot-inlined articles, so nothing was lost by moving this text:

- no Windows support, macOS and Linux only — `local-engineering.prompt`
  §"Tech Stack".
- do not modify SwarmForge itself, drive it from outside — `project.prompt`
  §"Constraints", which keeps that bullet.

The Constraints bullet that scoped the build to this milestone moved here with
it, verbatim, because its "described above" pointed at the charter below:

    - The full product vision (later milestones) is in `docs/reference/Specification.MD`
      and `docs/explanation/Milestone Roadmap.MD`. Build ONLY Milestone 1 described above.
      When in doubt, prefer the smaller scope.

`project.prompt` keeps the live half of it - both vision documents, and the
prefer-the-smaller-scope rule.

---

# Goals for this swarm run (Milestone 1 - the MVP)

Build the smallest version a developer would actually use daily. Deliver these,
and only these.

## Build order: two capabilities FIRST (the dogfood-critical pair)

Build these two before anything else, because together they let the developer
start using the extension to watch its own further development:

A. Run (launch) - a command to launch the swarm against a selected target by
   shelling out to SwarmForge's ./swarm wrapper.
B. Tiled agent panel - a webview panel showing one live terminal tile per agent
   role, each tailing that role's tmux pane in real time, each interactive (the
   developer can click in and type to the agent).

### DOGFOOD CHECKPOINT (important)

As soon as BOTH (A) launch and (B) live interactive tiles are working - even if
rough - PAUSE and surface a clearly visible message:

   "DOGFOOD CHECKPOINT REACHED - launch and tiles are functional. The developer
    should verify by pointing the extension at its own repo before continuing."

Do NOT stop the run. After surfacing this message, continue building the remaining
Milestone 1 items below. The checkpoint is a signal to the human, not a stopping
point - the human decides whether to take over watching via the extension itself.

## Remaining Milestone 1 items (build after the pair above)

1. Target selection
   - A command to point the extension at a local target repo (a filesystem path).
   - An "Initialize Target" command that scaffolds and commits two plain-text
     files into the target repo: project.prompt and engineering.prompt (so they
     travel with the repo when the swarm clones it).

2. Stop
   - A command to stop the swarm cleanly.

3. Pipeline awareness
   - Show which stage currently holds the work (read this from SwarmForge's state
     files under .swarmforge/). A simple status line is enough.

4. PR at the end
   - When the swarm finishes, open a pull request from the swarm's dev branch
     into the target's main branch. The human reviews and merges it in GitHub.

5. Named runs
   - Name the branch and PR after the work item. Keep a simple local log of runs.

Note: target selection (item 1) is needed to exercise launch properly, so it is
fine to build a minimal version of it alongside the dogfood pair if launch needs
a target to point at. Keep it minimal until the pair works.

# Explicitly OUT of scope (do NOT build these yet)

These are planned for later milestones. Do not start them now, even if they seem
useful - they would over-scope the MVP:

- No watchdog, heartbeat, or chase logic (rely on SwarmForge's own behavior and
  the human watching the tiles).
- No hardened message bus (read SwarmForge's existing state; do not redesign it).
- No completion-percentage "work tree", no backlog sync.
- No cost tracking or model/backend switching.
- No dynamic right-sizing of the pack (run whatever is configured).
- No per-tile respawn or model-switch controls.
- No remote access, phone app, or chat integration.
- No Windows support (macOS and Linux only - SwarmForge depends on tmux).

# Definition of done for this milestone

A developer can: select a target repo, run the swarm, watch all agents working in
live tiles inside VS Code, occasionally type a nudge to an agent, and end up with
a pull request to review - all without leaving the editor.
